1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,716
"Production rejects nothing when the question
is empty and the retriever falls over.

2
00:00:07,756 --> 00:00:08,840
I need a fix in ten minutes."

3
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:14,174
You're in the middle of a rebase, the
command that replays your commits onto a new

4
00:00:14,214 --> 00:00:19,023
base. It stopped at a conflict, and you've
already resolved two earlier ones by

5
00:00:19,063 --> 00:00:22,706
hand. You type git switch main.
Refused. You type git stash.

6
00:00:22,746 --> 00:00:27,371
It fails, because an index with unmerged
entries can't be written as a tree.

7
00:00:27,711 --> 00:00:33,601
Three options are left. Abort the rebase
and throw away the resolutions you've done.

8
00:00:33,641 --> 00:00:37,906
Clone the repository a second
time and wait for the download.

9
00:00:37,946 --> 00:00:43,836
Or read the second line of Git's own error
message, which says: Consider "git rebase

10
00:00:43,876 --> 00:00:45,953
--quit" or "git worktree add".

11
00:00:47,793 --> 00:00:51,388
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:51,428 --> 00:00:56,333
Last time you learned what a linked worktree
is on disk, a second working tree with

13
00:00:56,373 --> 00:01:01,339
its own HEAD and index on the same repository,
and why one branch can be checked out

14
00:01:01,379 --> 00:01:02,530
in only one of them.

15
00:01:02,870 --> 00:01:11,401
Today you use it: for the hotfix in the
hook, for reviewing a colleague's branch,

16
00:01:11,441 --> 00:01:19,972
and for parallel work by people or agents.
Then the life cycle, seven verbs: add,

17
00:01:20,012 --> 00:01:28,649
list, remove, lock, move, prune, repair.
And the surprises that come from the word

18
00:01:28,689 --> 00:01:29,601
"shared".

19
00:01:29,941 --> 00:01:35,065
One question to keep in mind: if you delete
a worktree's folder by hand, what does

20
00:01:35,105 --> 00:01:36,198
Git still believe?

21
00:01:38,038 --> 00:01:41,024
make an urgent fix in a second worktree
while a rebase is stopped in the first;

22
00:01:41,364 --> 00:01:44,331
review a colleague's branch
without disturbing your own work;

23
00:01:44,671 --> 00:01:53,697
add, list, remove, lock, move,
prune and repair worktrees;

24
00:01:54,037 --> 00:01:55,867
explain three surprises that come from
sharing: the stash, the configuration,

25
00:01:55,907 --> 00:01:56,207
ignored files;

26
00:01:56,547 --> 00:01:58,191
decide when a separate
clone is the better tool.

27
00:02:00,031 --> 00:02:03,500
Why a worktree solves the hotfix. The state
of a rebase lives in .git/rebase-merge/,

28
00:02:03,540 --> 00:02:06,926
and that is per worktree. Nothing that
happens in another worktree can disturb it.

29
00:02:06,966 --> 00:02:10,226
On screen, the first worktree of today's
demo: a conflict in the file, unmerged

30
00:02:10,266 --> 00:02:11,813
entries in the index, a detached HEAD.

31
00:02:12,153 --> 00:02:15,950
The hotfix branch, on the other
hand, is an ordinary shared ref.

32
00:02:15,990 --> 00:02:20,626
It starts at main, it gets the fix as a
commit, and it's still there after its

33
00:02:20,666 --> 00:02:21,645
worktree is gone.

34
00:02:21,985 --> 00:02:26,527
Reviewing. A detached worktree at a
remote-tracking branch, your record of a branch

35
00:02:26,567 --> 00:02:31,054
on the server, gives you the files to read,
run and test, without creating a local

36
00:02:31,094 --> 00:02:35,470
branch that you would have to delete later.
If you intend to push commits to the

37
00:02:35,510 --> 00:02:39,942
branch, name it instead: when no local
branch of that name exists and exactly one

38
00:02:39,982 --> 00:02:44,358
remote has one, git worktree add creates
a tracking branch, the same convenience

39
00:02:44,398 --> 00:02:45,628
that git switch offers.

40
00:02:45,968 --> 00:02:48,968
Parallel work. Each task gets
a branch and a directory.

41
00:02:49,008 --> 00:02:53,554
Compared with separate clones, the worktrees
share one object database, so a branch

42
00:02:53,594 --> 00:02:57,975
committed in one directory can be diffed,
merged or cherry-picked from any other

43
00:02:58,015 --> 00:03:02,507
immediately. The one-branch rule gives each
person or agent exclusive ownership of

44
00:03:02,547 --> 00:03:03,115
its branch.

45
00:03:03,455 --> 00:03:08,160
What you supply yourself: dependencies
and build output for each worktree.

46
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:12,905
Separate ports, caches and local
databases. An instruction never to stash.

47
00:03:12,945 --> 00:03:16,047
Integration, which is still
a merge. And cleanup.

48
00:03:16,387 --> 00:03:20,843
The life cycle. git worktree
add has three common forms.

49
00:03:20,883 --> 00:03:27,507
A path only: Git creates a new branch named
after the last path component, starting

50
00:03:27,547 --> 00:03:34,010
at HEAD. That default surprises people who
meant "give me a copy of what I have".

51
00:03:34,050 --> 00:03:38,266
With -b, an explicit new
branch and a starting point.

52
00:03:38,306 --> 00:03:40,835
With --detach, no branch at all.

53
00:03:41,175 --> 00:03:44,212
🟡 CAUTION: git worktree remove deletes
the working tree directory and its entry

54
00:03:44,252 --> 00:03:47,250
under .git/worktrees/, including its HEAD
reflog. It refuses when the tree has

55
00:03:47,290 --> 00:03:50,172
modified or untracked files. Removing
a worktree doesn't delete its branch.

56
00:03:50,512 --> 00:03:53,238
git worktree lock protects a worktree
from prune, move and remove.

57
00:03:53,278 --> 00:03:56,256
git worktree move relocates a linked
worktree and updates both pointers.

58
00:03:56,296 --> 00:03:59,316
It can't move the main worktree, nor
a worktree that contains submodules.

59
00:03:59,656 --> 00:04:02,669
If someone removes the directory with rm
-rf, the entry remains, and with it the

60
00:04:02,709 --> 00:04:05,416
claim on the branch. 🟡 CAUTION: git
worktree prune removes such entries.

61
00:04:05,456 --> 00:04:08,355
If the directory was moved with mv, one
of the two pointers is stale, and git

62
00:04:08,395 --> 00:04:10,186
worktree repair, which is
🟢 SAFE, rewrites them.

63
00:04:10,526 --> 00:04:15,287
The pitfalls of sharing. Ignored files don't
come along: a worktree is a checkout of

64
00:04:15,327 --> 00:04:18,487
a commit. Stashes are shared:
refs/stash is under refs/.

65
00:04:18,527 --> 00:04:22,658
Configuration and hooks are shared:
git config set without a scope writes

66
00:04:22,698 --> 00:04:27,002
.git/config, which every worktree reads.
Per-worktree configuration needs an

67
00:04:27,042 --> 00:04:29,459
extension that has to
be switched on first.

68
00:04:29,799 --> 00:04:34,133
When not to use a worktree.
Section 25.9 lists four cases.

69
00:04:34,173 --> 00:04:40,392
For a two-minute look at another branch
when your working tree is clean: git switch

70
00:04:40,432 --> 00:04:46,727
is enough. To get isolation: worktrees share
refs, stashes, configuration and hooks,

71
00:04:46,767 --> 00:04:52,987
so for an untrusted experiment, a destructive
history rewrite you want to rehearse,

72
00:04:53,027 --> 00:04:58,115
or a different set of remotes and
credentials, use a separate clone.

73
00:04:58,455 --> 00:05:03,820
For a superproject with submodules, unless
you have tested your exact workflow: the

74
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:09,095
manual advises against it. And as a
substitute for branches: ten worktrees on ten

75
00:05:09,135 --> 00:05:11,569
stale branches are ten stale branches.

76
00:05:13,409 --> 00:05:17,841
Keep the library with several desks.
Today's additions are about the furniture.

77
00:05:18,181 --> 00:05:24,117
A desk can be added and taken away
without touching the shelves.

78
00:05:24,157 --> 00:05:31,773
Taking a desk away doesn't un-shelve the
books written at it, as long as they were

79
00:05:31,813 --> 00:05:39,336
catalogued under a branch. But notes that
lay on the desk and were never shelved,

80
00:05:39,376 --> 00:05:45,405
the untracked and modified files,
go with the desk if you insist.

81
00:05:45,745 --> 00:05:50,373
The library keeps a register of its desks.
If a desk is carried out of the building

82
00:05:50,413 --> 00:05:54,928
without telling the librarian, the register
still lists it and still reserves its

83
00:05:54,968 --> 00:05:56,446
branch. That is "prunable".

84
00:05:56,786 --> 00:06:02,212
And three things sit at the front counter,
not at any desk: the stash drawer, the

85
00:06:02,252 --> 00:06:06,126
house rules, and the hooks.
Every desk uses the same ones.

86
00:06:06,166 --> 00:06:11,525
The analogy breaks if you take it to mean
that the desks are independent copies.

87
00:06:11,565 --> 00:06:17,059
They aren't, and deleting the main worktree's
directory deletes the repository for

88
00:06:17,099 --> 00:06:17,868
all of them.

89
00:06:19,708 --> 00:06:27,094
Try it now, on paper. Draw one box for the
repository and three for its worktrees.

90
00:06:27,134 --> 00:06:33,432
Where do these go: the stash, the
index, the rebase state, the config?

91
00:06:33,472 --> 00:06:37,959
Pause me for thirty seconds
and write your answer.

92
00:06:42,799 --> 00:06:47,733
Point at rebase-merge/ in the left box:
that is why the hotfix can't disturb the

93
00:06:47,773 --> 00:06:52,893
rebase. Point at refs/stash and config in
the top box: that is where two of today's

94
00:06:52,933 --> 00:06:56,934
surprises come from. Point at the
.venv lines: that is the third.

95
00:06:58,774 --> 00:07:03,533
Both escape routes are closed, and the
refusal of git switch names the alternative.

96
00:07:03,573 --> 00:07:07,869
🟢 SAFE: git worktree add -b creates a
directory, an entry and a new branch.

97
00:07:08,209 --> 00:07:11,283
-b creates the branch at main and
checks it out in the new directory.

98
00:07:11,323 --> 00:07:14,893
The list shows the main worktree as
"(detached HEAD)": that is the rebase, which

99
00:07:14,933 --> 00:07:16,834
works on a detached
HEAD until it finishes.

100
00:07:20,174 --> 00:07:30,066
The fix is written, checked, committed
and pushed from the second directory.

101
00:07:30,106 --> 00:07:38,691
Now go back. Predict what git status
shows in the first directory.

102
00:07:38,731 --> 00:07:42,220
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

103
00:07:53,060 --> 00:07:58,933
The rebase is exactly where you left it,
UU app/retriever.py, and the hotfix branch

104
00:07:58,973 --> 00:08:02,566
is in the shared refs with
its upstream configured.

105
00:08:07,406 --> 00:08:10,873
A detached worktree at 00daaec. You
read, run and test there, and your own

106
00:08:10,913 --> 00:08:14,570
modification is untouched throughout.
And if you intend to push commits to the

107
00:08:14,610 --> 00:08:14,902
branch:

108
00:08:15,242 --> 00:08:17,253
"new branch 'feature/citations'"
and "set up to track".

109
00:08:17,593 --> 00:08:22,668
Look at the first form: a path only, and
Git says "new branch 'spike-cache'".

110
00:08:22,708 --> 00:08:27,253
You didn't ask for a branch. This
one surprises almost everyone once.

111
00:08:27,593 --> 00:08:31,421
Use --porcelain in scripts. The manual
promises that this format stays stable.

112
00:08:31,761 --> 00:08:36,136
The plain form refused. After the forced
removal the worktree is gone and its branch

113
00:08:36,176 --> 00:08:38,238
isn't. The branch is deleted separately.

114
00:08:38,578 --> 00:08:41,942
A lock with a reason, and the
refusal quotes the reason.

115
00:08:41,982 --> 00:08:43,705
🟡 CAUTION: git worktree move.

116
00:08:44,045 --> 00:08:48,325
Now the directory deleted by hand,
the question from the start.

117
00:08:48,365 --> 00:08:53,057
Quick quiz, two options. After the
rm -rf, its branch can be deleted.

118
00:08:53,097 --> 00:08:55,045
Or it can't. Say it out loud.

119
00:08:59,885 --> 00:09:06,537
It can't. "prunable" in the listing, and
the branch is still "used by worktree at" a

120
00:09:06,577 --> 00:09:12,672
path that no longer exists. The dry run
says why: the gitdir file points to a

121
00:09:12,712 --> 00:09:17,690
non-existent location. git worktree
prune removes such entries.

122
00:09:17,730 --> 00:09:23,904
Git also prunes stale entries by itself:
git gc uses a default expiry of three

123
00:09:23,944 --> 00:09:29,481
months, and the automatic maintenance
of Git 2.54 and later includes a

124
00:09:29,521 --> 00:09:31,074
worktree-prune task.

125
00:09:31,414 --> 00:09:32,411
Moved by hand:

126
00:09:32,751 --> 00:09:39,127
The repair message names worktrees/rag-api-v1.3.0:
the worktree's ID, the directory

127
00:09:39,167 --> 00:09:45,001
name it was created with. The ID doesn't
change when the working tree moves.

128
00:09:45,341 --> 00:09:47,564
And the main worktree itself renamed:

129
00:09:47,904 --> 00:10:19,053
"not a git repository" in the linked worktree,
until git worktree repair runs in the

130
00:10:19,093 --> 00:10:22,395
main one.

131
00:10:22,735 --> 00:10:26,211
The virtual environment is ignored, so it
is in no commit, so it isn't in the new

132
00:10:26,251 --> 00:10:26,602
worktree.

133
00:10:26,942 --> 00:10:29,546
Predict: the stash was made
in the other directory.

134
00:10:29,586 --> 00:10:31,360
Is it listed here? Say it out loud.

135
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:44,598
Yes. One stash list for the whole repository.
A git stash pop here would apply an

136
00:10:44,638 --> 00:10:50,234
entry that was made on another
branch in another directory.

137
00:10:50,574 --> 00:10:52,964
A setting made "for that
worktree" changed all of them.

138
00:10:53,004 --> 00:10:55,040
Per-worktree configuration
needs the extension.

139
00:10:55,380 --> 00:10:57,732
--worktree is refused until
extensions.worktreeConfig is true.

140
00:10:57,772 --> 00:11:00,819
The manual notes that older Git versions
refuse to work in a repository that has

141
00:11:00,859 --> 00:11:01,552
this extension set.

142
00:11:01,892 --> 00:11:05,246
One reassurance: garbage collection
respects other worktrees.

143
00:11:05,586 --> 00:11:08,794
A commit held only by the detached HEAD
of another worktree survives git gc

144
00:11:08,834 --> 00:11:11,912
--prune=now. That protection lasts as
long as the worktree's entry does.

145
00:11:11,952 --> 00:11:15,420
After remove or prune, a commit made on a
detached HEAD there is held by nothing,

146
00:11:15,460 --> 00:11:17,802
because the worktree's HEAD
reflog went with the entry.

147
00:11:17,842 --> 00:11:20,357
Put work on a branch before you
remove a detached worktree.

148
00:11:22,197 --> 00:11:23,845
Five mistakes to watch for.

149
00:11:24,185 --> 00:11:28,978
Deleting a worktree directory with
rm -rf. Root cause: the entry under

150
00:11:29,018 --> 00:11:34,709
.git/worktrees/ remains and keeps its claim
on the branch until git worktree prune.

151
00:11:35,049 --> 00:11:39,561
git worktree add ../name to "copy what I
have". Root cause: with only a path, Git

152
00:11:39,601 --> 00:11:42,764
creates a new branch named
after the last path component.

153
00:11:43,104 --> 00:11:46,798
git stash pop in a repository
with several worktrees.

154
00:11:46,838 --> 00:11:52,433
Root cause: refs/stash is under refs/ and
therefore shared; the newest entry may

155
00:11:52,473 --> 00:11:54,053
belong to another task.

156
00:11:54,393 --> 00:11:59,421
Setting configuration "for this
worktree" with a plain git config set.

157
00:11:59,461 --> 00:12:05,501
Root cause: it writes .git/config, which
every worktree reads; per-worktree settings

158
00:12:05,541 --> 00:12:08,831
need extensions.worktreeConfig
and --worktree.

159
00:12:09,171 --> 00:12:19,201
Removing a detached worktree that holds
commits. Root cause: the commits were named

160
00:12:19,241 --> 00:12:29,150
only by that worktree's HEAD and HEAD
reflog, and both are deleted with the entry.

161
00:12:30,990 --> 00:12:35,280
Now, out of the lab. A team runs three
coding agents on one service repository.

162
00:12:35,320 --> 00:12:38,185
Each task gets a worktree
and a branch with a prefix.

163
00:12:38,225 --> 00:12:42,625
The setup script that creates a worktree
also creates a virtual environment in it

164
00:12:42,665 --> 00:12:47,010
and assigns a port, because ignored files,
ports and caches are outside what Git

165
00:12:47,050 --> 00:12:48,106
shares or separates.

166
00:12:48,446 --> 00:12:53,533
The agents' instructions contain one Git
rule from section 25.5: commit to your

167
00:12:53,573 --> 00:12:58,790
branch, and never stash, because an agent
that runs git stash pop may pop another

168
00:12:58,830 --> 00:13:04,112
agent's stash. Integration is an ordinary
merge, and two branches that touched the

169
00:13:04,152 --> 00:13:09,564
same lines conflict as they always would.
A cleanup job runs git worktree remove for

170
00:13:09,604 --> 00:13:15,015
each finished directory and git branch -d
for each merged branch, and nobody deletes

171
00:13:15,055 --> 00:13:16,313
directories by hand.

172
00:13:16,653 --> 00:13:21,069
For one task the team uses a separate
clone on purpose: rehearsing a history

173
00:13:21,109 --> 00:13:25,995
rewrite. Worktrees share refs, and a rehearsal
that rewrites refs isn't something to

174
00:13:26,035 --> 00:13:26,347
share.

175
00:13:28,187 --> 00:13:34,962
Your turn. Do Lab 14.1, "A hotfix in a
second worktree while a rebase is in

176
00:13:35,002 --> 00:13:38,778
progress", in lab-manual/m14-worktrees.md.

177
00:13:39,118 --> 00:13:42,804
Before you add the worktree, predict what
git worktree list will show for the first

178
00:13:42,844 --> 00:13:46,529
directory while the rebase is stopped.
Before you return, predict the output of git

179
00:13:46,569 --> 00:13:50,255
status --short there. The lab's failure
scenario is the cleanup mistake people make

180
00:13:50,295 --> 00:13:53,397
afterwards. Predict which command
will refuse, and with which message.

181
00:13:53,737 --> 00:13:59,553
The challenge is Exercise 14.9, Level 4,
"Two fixes in a worktree that no longer

182
00:13:59,593 --> 00:14:03,652
exists", in
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

183
00:14:05,492 --> 00:14:07,423
Answer out loud. I'll wait.

184
00:14:12,263 --> 00:14:17,468
A strong answer first explains why the two
everyday reflexes are unavailable in this

185
00:14:17,508 --> 00:14:22,588
state. It then compares the three options
on the same criteria: what is lost, what

186
00:14:22,628 --> 00:14:27,458
it costs in time and disk, what has to
be kept in sync afterwards, and what is

187
00:14:27,498 --> 00:14:27,895
shared.

188
00:14:28,235 --> 00:14:31,720
It explains why the rebase survives next
to a worktree, in terms of where its state

189
00:14:31,760 --> 00:14:35,244
is stored. It doesn't present the worktree
as free of trade-offs: it names what the

190
00:14:35,284 --> 00:14:38,684
worktree shares that a clone doesn't, and
gives a situation in which the clone is

191
00:14:38,724 --> 00:14:39,406
the right choice.

192
00:14:41,246 --> 00:14:44,173
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

193
00:14:44,513 --> 00:14:49,475
A stopped rebase stays intact while I fix
something in another worktree, because the

194
00:14:49,515 --> 00:14:53,286
rebase state is per worktree and
the new branch is a shared ref.

195
00:14:53,626 --> 00:14:58,341
For review I add a detached worktree at
the remote-tracking branch, or name the

196
00:14:58,381 --> 00:15:00,026
branch if I will push to it.

197
00:15:00,366 --> 00:15:05,344
I remove worktrees with git worktree
remove, clean up after manual deletion with

198
00:15:05,384 --> 00:15:07,979
prune, and after manual moves with repair.

199
00:15:08,319 --> 00:15:11,852
Ignored files are not in a new worktree;
the stash list, the configuration and the

200
00:15:11,892 --> 00:15:13,247
hooks are shared by all of them.

201
00:15:13,587 --> 00:15:15,784
When I need isolation,
I use a separate clone.

202
00:15:17,624 --> 00:15:26,249
Read sections 25.5 to 25.10 of Chapter
25 and do the Practice section, 25.12.

203
00:15:26,589 --> 00:15:32,572
Today you fixed production without giving
up a half-finished rebase, and you know

204
00:15:32,612 --> 00:15:38,373
the seven verbs of a worktree's life. Do
the hotfix lab before the next video.

205
00:15:38,413 --> 00:15:44,173
Next time: stash internals, where a stash
entry turns out to be a small commit

206
00:15:44,213 --> 00:15:48,561
graph. Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

207
00:15:48,601 --> 00:15:50,346
See you in the next one.
